\begin{didyouknow}{A Theorem Behind Bisection}
Bisection works because a continuous function that changes sign must cross zero somewhere in between. That sounds obvious, but Bernard Bolzano gave one of the first rigorous proofs only in 1817. Each bisection step halves the interval, so ten steps shrink it by a factor $2^{10}=1024$, gaining about three decimal digits. The method is slow, but once a sign change is bracketed it never fails.
\end{didyouknow}
% DYK-END

