\begin{didyouknow}{Newton's Favorite Cubic}
Isaac Newton illustrated his root-finding method around 1669 on the equation $y^3-2y-5=0$, and Joseph Raphson published a streamlined version in 1690. Near a simple root the method roughly doubles the number of correct digits at each step. Much earlier, Heron of Alexandria described an iteration for square roots that is exactly Newton's method applied to $x^2-a=0$.
\end{didyouknow}
